WEBVTT

1
00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:03.791
<b>In the previous verse, Krishna explained</b>

2
00:01:03.791 --> 00:01:10.375
<b>that focused meditation on Him is the </b>
<b>path to attaining steady wisdom.</b>

3
00:01:10.375 --> 00:01:13.250
<b>But what happens if we don't follow this path?</b>

4
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:19.958
<b>if we fail to fully protect our senses and </b>
<b>attention from worldly attachments,</b>

5
00:01:19.958 --> 00:01:22.458
<b>what are the consequences of it?</b>

6
00:01:22.458 --> 00:01:25.916
<b>Here, Krishna elaborates on this.</b>

7
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.083
<b>Krishna explains here </b>
<b>the adverse consequences of</b>

8
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:44.250
<b>attempting to control only the senses without </b>
<b>focusing the mind on Him through concentration.</b>

9
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:51.541
<b>Trying to control just the senses without</b>
<b>disciplining the mind can be a grave mistake.</b>

10
00:01:51.541 --> 00:01:53.458
<b>This control might seem simple,</b>

11
00:01:53.458 --> 00:01:59.208
<b>but even a small thought </b>
<b>can completely destroy it.</b>

12
00:01:59.208 --> 00:02:03.166
<b>Krishna explains this through eight (8) </b>
<b>sequential steps:</b>

13
00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:04.500
<b>First</b>

14
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.541
<b>It begins with merely thinking about an object</b>

15
00:02:09.541 --> 00:02:10.750
<b>Second</b>

16
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.416
<b>Continuous thinking soon </b>
<b>transforms into attachment</b>

17
00:02:15.416 --> 00:02:16.666
<b>Third</b>

18
00:02:16.666 --> 00:02:22.291
<b>This attachment gives birth to an </b>
<b>intense desire to possess that object</b>

19
00:02:22.291 --> 00:02:23.708
<b>Fourth</b>

20
00:02:23.708 --> 00:02:30.041
<b>When this desire appears unfulfilled, </b>
<b>it gives rise to anger</b>

21
00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:31.125
<b>Fifth</b>

22
00:02:31.125 --> 00:02:36.416
<b>Anger is destructive. It confuses the person</b>

23
00:02:36.416 --> 00:02:37.500
<b>Sixth</b>

24
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:43.125
<b>The confusion born of this </b>
<b>anger damages one's memory. </b>

25
00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:47.541
<b>They forget the distinction between</b>
<b>right and wrong</b>

26
00:02:47.541 --> 00:02:49.083
<b>Seventh</b>

27
00:02:49.083 --> 00:02:55.583
<b>This forgetting of the distinction between </b>
<b>right and wrong is the destruction of intelligence</b>

28
00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:56.666
<b>Eighth</b>

29
00:02:56.666 --> 00:03:00.291
<b>Finally, one whose intelligence </b>
<b>is thus destroyed continuously</b>

30
00:03:00.291 --> 00:03:03.458
<b>makes unethical decisions, </b>

31
00:03:03.458 --> 00:03:05.916
<b>choosing flawed options.</b>

32
00:03:05.916 --> 00:03:10.583
<b>This becomes the cause of that person's downfall</b>

33
00:03:10.583 --> 00:03:11.583
<b>Therefore,</b>

34
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:18.375
<b>the rigid notion of controlling</b>
<b>only the senses is actually a mistake </b>

35
00:03:18.375 --> 00:03:22.041
<b>that can prove harmful later.</b>

36
00:03:22.041 --> 00:03:28.041
<b>After controlling the senses, it's essential</b>
<b>to direct them in the right direction.</b>

37
00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:28.791
<b>And</b>

38
00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:36.166
<b>what could be better than focusing them </b>
<b>in concentrated meditation on Lord Krishna?</b>

39
00:03:40.708 --> 00:03:43.833
<b>Perhaps a doubt arises in your mind</b>

40
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:50.416
<b>can a mere thought become </b>
<b>the cause of one's downfall?</b>

41
00:03:50.416 --> 00:03:53.958
<b>The brief answer is….</b>

42
00:03:53.958 --> 00:03:55.708
<b>Yes.</b>

43
00:03:55.708 --> 00:03:59.083
<b>Let me explain this in detail:</b>

44
00:03:59.083 --> 00:04:02.958
<b>If a crack appears in the foundation</b>
<b>of a magnificent building,</b>

45
00:04:02.958 --> 00:04:05.208
<b>and if left unattended,</b>

46
00:04:05.208 --> 00:04:10.916
<b>this tiny crack could cause the entire </b>
<b>structure to collapse tomorrow.</b>

47
00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:12.541
<b>The building was massive,</b>

48
00:04:12.541 --> 00:04:14.375
<b>the crack minute,</b>

49
00:04:14.375 --> 00:04:16.625
<b>yet this happened.</b>

50
00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:18.166
<b>Similarly,</b>

51
00:04:18.166 --> 00:04:25.708
<b>a single small thought can</b>
<b>cause a person's downfall.</b>

52
00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:26.708
<b>But</b>

53
00:04:26.708 --> 00:04:31.125
<b>where do these thoughts originate?</b>

54
00:04:31.125 --> 00:04:33.291
<b>From our senses,</b>

55
00:04:33.291 --> 00:04:35.208
<b>from their experiences.</b>

56
00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:42.708
<b>Seeing, hearing, tasting, touching, and smelling</b>

57
00:04:42.708 --> 00:04:49.083
<b>all five senses are capable </b>
<b>of generating thoughts.</b>

58
00:04:49.083 --> 00:04:50.375
<b>However,</b>

59
00:04:50.375 --> 00:04:55.583
<b>among these five, the sense </b>
<b>of sight is most active,</b>

60
00:04:55.583 --> 00:05:00.750
<b>and therefore can prove most dangerous.</b>

61
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.166
<b>When someone sees something beautiful, </b>

62
00:05:03.166 --> 00:05:07.166
<b>they try to see it again and again.</b>

63
00:05:07.166 --> 00:05:10.291
<b>This is the beginning of attraction,</b>

64
00:05:10.291 --> 00:05:15.708
<b>which eventually takes </b>
<b>complete control over the person.</b>

65
00:05:15.708 --> 00:05:21.333
<b>Then they cannot live without </b>
<b>possessing that object.</b>

66
00:05:21.333 --> 00:05:27.125
<b>The world of advertising thrives on </b>
<b>this human weakness.</b>

67
00:05:27.125 --> 00:05:30.333
<b>When you repeatedly see an advertisement,</b>

68
00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:34.458
<b>you become enchanted by that product,</b>

69
00:05:34.458 --> 00:05:40.833
<b>creating an intense desire to</b>
<b>possess it, and ultimately</b>

70
00:05:40.833 --> 00:05:44.166
<b>you end up buying it.</b>

71
00:05:44.166 --> 00:05:45.458
<b>Then </b>

72
00:05:45.458 --> 00:05:47.250
<b>another advertisement, </b>

73
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.666
<b>another attraction, </b>

74
00:05:48.666 --> 00:05:50.583
<b>another desire.</b>

75
00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:53.583
<b>The cycle continues.</b>

76
00:05:53.583 --> 00:05:57.833
<b>Don't underestimate the power of thoughts.</b>

77
00:05:57.833 --> 00:06:03.416
<b>Don't undervalue the destructive </b>
<b>potential of the senses.</b>

78
00:06:03.416 --> 00:06:05.250
<b>Stay vigilant.</b>

79
00:06:09.875 --> 00:06:14.791
<b>Society has witnessed many heinous crimes.</b>

80
00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:16.625
<b>What happens</b>

81
00:06:16.625 --> 00:06:18.541
<b>to criminals</b>

82
00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:25.875
<b>that without considering others'</b>
<b>destruction or even their own</b>

83
00:06:25.875 --> 00:06:29.875
<b>makes them commit such acts?</b>

84
00:06:29.875 --> 00:06:34.916
<b>Is it merely a moment's mistake?</b>

85
00:06:34.916 --> 00:06:37.458
<b>Certainly not.</b>

86
00:06:37.458 --> 00:06:39.666
<b>No act,</b>

87
00:06:39.666 --> 00:06:41.791
<b>whether good</b>

88
00:06:41.791 --> 00:06:45.166
<b>or utterly condemnable,</b>

89
00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:51.291
<b>reaches its peak in an instant.</b>

90
00:06:51.291 --> 00:06:55.416
<b>Behind every crime lies a sequence.</b>

91
00:06:55.416 --> 00:07:00.041
<b>The seed of that crime was planted</b>

92
00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:05.333
<b>when a distortion entered the criminal's mind,</b>

93
00:07:05.333 --> 00:07:11.500
<b>and that small distortion grew </b>
<b>until it overwhelmed them,</b>

94
00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:16.833
<b>destroying their ability to distinguish </b>
<b>right from wrong.</b>

95
00:07:16.833 --> 00:07:25.083
<b>The crime is merely the visible </b>
<b>outcome of this sequence.</b>

96
00:07:25.083 --> 00:07:27.166
<b>Do you think</b>

97
00:07:27.166 --> 00:07:28.375
<b>violence,</b>

98
00:07:28.375 --> 00:07:29.500
<b>fraud,</b>

99
00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.458
<b>sexual crimes</b>

100
00:07:31.458 --> 00:07:34.916
<b>these happen without any thought?</b>

101
00:07:34.916 --> 00:07:36.958
<b>Certainly not.</b>

102
00:07:36.958 --> 00:07:41.750
<b>It always begins with a thought,</b>

103
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.416
<b>and then</b>

104
00:07:43.416 --> 00:07:47.333
<b>its constant repetition.</b>

105
00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:50.375
<b>When someone becomes enchanted by wealth,</b>

106
00:07:50.375 --> 00:07:55.291
<b>overwhelmed by a life of pleasure and luxury,</b>

107
00:07:55.291 --> 00:07:58.458
<b>this attachment doesn't stop.</b>

108
00:07:58.458 --> 00:08:02.125
<b>In the desire to amass more wealth in less time,</b>

109
00:08:02.125 --> 00:08:08.916
<b>such a person doesn't hesitate </b>
<b>to commit unethical acts.</b>

110
00:08:08.916 --> 00:08:13.166
<b>For everyone's well-being, for society's welfare,</b>

111
00:08:13.166 --> 00:08:21.666
<b>it's essential that we all together make </b>
<b>the purification of thoughts our personal mission.</b>

112
00:08:21.666 --> 00:08:23.833
<b>Practice it yourself,</b>

113
00:08:23.833 --> 00:08:26.291
<b>inspire others to do the same.</b>

114
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.208
<b>Insulting someone in anger,</b>

115
00:08:34.208 --> 00:08:39.000
<b>doing something in rage to </b>
<b>teach someone a lesson</b>

116
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.291
<b>only to regret it later.</b>

117
00:08:42.291 --> 00:08:45.791
<b>This isn't uncommon in professional life;</b>

118
00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:48.250
<b>you must have witnessed it,</b>

119
00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:49.541
<b>perhaps</b>

120
00:08:49.541 --> 00:08:53.833
<b>experienced it yourself.</b>

121
00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:56.958
<b>When anger overtakes someone,</b>

122
00:08:56.958 --> 00:09:02.291
<b>they lose the ability to think clearly.</b>

123
00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:06.125
<b>They blame the person who triggered their anger,</b>

124
00:09:06.125 --> 00:09:08.625
<b>blame their behavior,</b>

125
00:09:08.625 --> 00:09:11.250
<b>blame office politics</b>

126
00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:17.625
<b>they consider everyone guilty except themselves.</b>

127
00:09:17.625 --> 00:09:20.583
<b>But is this right?</b>

128
00:09:20.583 --> 00:09:23.666
<b>Is it solely the fault of someone's behavior? </b>

129
00:09:23.666 --> 00:09:27.041
<b>Of office politics?</b>

130
00:09:27.041 --> 00:09:28.458
<b>No.</b>

131
00:09:28.458 --> 00:09:34.125
<b>These merely bring hidden rage to the surface.</b>

132
00:09:34.125 --> 00:09:37.416
<b>They aren't the root cause of anger.</b>

133
00:09:37.416 --> 00:09:38.541
<b>But</b>

134
00:09:38.541 --> 00:09:44.916
<b>then what is the root cause of anger and rage?</b>

135
00:09:44.916 --> 00:09:48.041
<b>A person's unfulfilled desires,</b>

136
00:09:48.041 --> 00:09:51.458
<b>their unmet expectations.</b>

137
00:09:51.458 --> 00:09:57.291
<b>When one's desires and expectations </b>
<b>aren't met according to their wishes,</b>

138
00:09:57.291 --> 00:10:01.291
<b>it generates anger.</b>

139
00:10:01.291 --> 00:10:05.541
<b>In professional life, everyone </b>
<b>has their own perspective,</b>

140
00:10:05.541 --> 00:10:08.000
<b>their own working method,</b>

141
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.666
<b>their own difficulties.</b>

142
00:10:10.666 --> 00:10:11.875
<b>But</b>

143
00:10:11.875 --> 00:10:16.000
<b>a slave to expectations denies all these,</b>

144
00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.000
<b>wanting others to work only </b>
<b>according to their way,</b>

145
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.208
<b>and whoever doesn't is wrong,</b>

146
00:10:24.208 --> 00:10:26.833
<b>deserving of anger.</b>

147
00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:32.708
<b>This mindset is harmful in professional life. </b>
<b>Control it.</b>

148
00:10:32.708 --> 00:10:36.208
<b>Make your expectations realistic,</b>

149
00:10:36.208 --> 00:10:41.333
<b>try to understand others' perspectives </b>
<b>and their working methods.</b>

150
00:10:41.333 --> 00:10:46.250
<b>Doing this will help you control your impulses.</b>

151
00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:49.208
<b>Free yourself from anger's control.</b>

152
00:10:49.208 --> 00:10:53.041
<b>Lead a better professional life.</b>

